## 1. Task 1 — Multi-Digit Column Arithmetic with Carry

### 1.1 Why this task has high 2D treewidth

Lay the two operands as a 2×L grid, digit `A[i]` at position `(0, L-1-i)`, `B[i]` at `(1, L-1-i)`. The column-wise sum at position `(·, j)` depends on the carry from column `j-1`, which in turn depends on column `j-2`, …, transitively on **all** columns to the right. Formally, the primal graph of the addition circuit has an induced path along the column axis whose elimination necessarily creates a bag containing all active carry states. For a carry that is "live" over a run of length `c`, the pathwidth contribution along the column axis is Θ(c).

Under a row-major flattening (which every 1D NCA on this task must use; there is no alternative linear embedding that does not interleave the two operands), the carry at column `j` sits at linear position `2(L-1-j)` and the operand digits it must combine with sit at positions `2(L-1-j)` and `2(L-1-j)+1`, while the carry that produced it sits at `2(L-j)`. Because the carry travels along the column axis and the digits along the row axis, the 1D flattening must alternate between adjacencies that are spatially local in exactly one dimension. Any consistent total order forces a **cut** of size ≥ 2 (operand axis × column axis) across which information must propagate. The dependency graph therefore has **linear cutwidth**, and no 1D neighborhood of fixed radius ±k can carry the carry signal from column `j` to column `j+k` without k intermediate steps — but at column j+k, the signal has already been consumed.

This is the concrete adversarial case for 1D: when a long carry chain (e.g., `999…99 + 1`) spans c columns, the 1D NCA must traverse 2c positions — but the digits it needs at the far end have already been passed. Macro Recursive Feedback cannot fix this: the global pool destroys *position*, so the broadcast bias is the same at every column and cannot encode "carry is still propagating."

### 1.2 Input encoding

Grid shape `(2, L)` plus a separate binary carry-in plane is **not** provided (that is cheating). The model must learn to produce carries.

Channel layout (C_in = 4):

| ch | meaning |
|----|---------|
| 0 | one-hot digit of A, but one-hot over L is infeasible; use **one-hot over 10** ∈ ℝ^10 |
| 0..9 | A digit one-hot |
| 10..19 | B digit one-hot |
| 20 | validity mask (1 if column exists else 0) |
| 21 | constant 1 (bias plane, helps matching) |

So C_in = 22, grid `(2, L)`.

### 1.3 Target encoding

Target is the sum represented as `(1, L+1)` grid of one-hot digits over 10 plus a leading column. Loss is per-cell cross-entropy on the 10-way digit class, masked by validity. This makes the task an **iterative refinement** problem: NCA must emit correct per-digit carry states; the final digit at column `j` is a function of `A[j]`, `B[j]`, carry_in[j].

